'''''Calendula arvensis''''' is a species of flowering plant in the [[Asteraceae|daisy family]] known by the common name '''field marigold'''. It is native to central and southern Europe, North Africa and the Middle East<ref>{{cite web |title=Calendula arvensis L. {{!}} Plants of the World Online {{!}} Kew Science |url=https://powo.science.kew.org/taxon/urn:lsid:ipni.org:names:187819-1 |website=Plants of the World Online |access-date=10 November 2024 |language=en}}</ref> and it is known across the globe as an [[introduced species]].<ref>[http://www.efloras.org/florataxon.aspx?flora_id=1&taxon_id=250005003 Flora of North America, ''Calendula arvensis'' Linnaeus, Sp. Pl. Ed. 2,. 2: 1303. 1763. ]</ref><ref>[http://luirig.altervista.org/flora/taxa/index1.php?scientific-name=calendula+arvensis Altervista Flora Italiana, Fiorrancio dei campi ''Calendula arvensis'' (Vaill.) L.] includes photos and European distribution map</ref>

''Calendula arvensis'' is an [[annual plant|annual]] or [[biennial plant|biennial]] [[herbaceous plant|herb]] {{Convert|10|to|50|cm|abbr=on|sp=us|in}} tall. The leaves are lance-shaped and borne on [[Petiole (botany)|petioles]] from the slender, hairy stem. The [[inflorescence]] is a single [[Head (botany)|flower head]] up to four centimeters wide with bright yellow to yellow-orange ray florets around a center of yellow disc florets. The fruit is an [[achene]] which can take any of three shapes, including ring-shaped, that facilitate different methods of [[biological dispersal|dispersal]].<ref>Ruiz de Clavijo, E. (2005). The reproductive strategies of the heterocarpic annual ''Calendula arvensis'' (Asteraceae). ''Acta Oecologica'' 28:2 119-26.</ref><ref name="Acta">{{cite web |first1=Nino |last1=Messina |title=''Calendula arvensis'' |year=2010 |website=Actaplantarum |url=http://www.actaplantarum.org/floraitaliae/viewtopic.php?f=95&t=15394 |accessdate=21 January 2015 |archive-date=25 March 2015 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20150325200650/http://www.actaplantarum.org/floraitaliae/viewtopic.php?f=95&t=15394 |url-status=dead }}</ref>

==Achene==

'''''Calendula arvensis''''' produce three types of achenes (fruits of the sunflower family), they are rostrate, cymbiform and annular. Rostrate and cymbiform are suitable for long-distance diffusion, because they have larger size and weight than annular, while annular is suitable for short-distance diffusion.<ref>{{Cite journal |last=Ruiz De Clavijo |first=E. |date=2005-09-01 |title=The reproductive strategies of the heterocarpic annual Calendula arvensis (Asteraceae) |url=https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S1146609X05000275 |journal=Acta Oecologica |language=en |volume=28 |issue=2 |pages=119–126 |doi=10.1016/j.actao.2005.03.004 |issn=1146-609X|url-access=subscription }}</ref>

